BT /F1 7 Tf 8.4 TL 28 812 Td (Textdruck des Hauptbereichs \(article\) durch KAP3, kein Bilddruck; Abbildungen nur mit Unterschrift.) Tj T* (Quelle: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9639227/ \(abgerufen 27.09.2026 06:56 UTC, HTTP 200, SHA-256 der Rohdatei 4df6e16c8ac934b40220b4b8ea29) Tj T* (  f55ef85dd35e571bd1e3d67a727154a2ca9d\).) Tj T* (Rohdatei daneben: 11_Winklmayr2022_Hitzemortalitaet_PMC9639227.html) Tj T* () Tj T* (Dtsch Arztebl Int) Tj T* (. 2022 Jul 1;119\(26\):451–457. doi: 10.3238/arztebl.m2022.0202) Tj T* (Search in PMC) Tj T* (Search in PubMed) Tj T* (View in NLM Catalog) Tj T* (Add to search) Tj T* (Heat-Related Mortality in Germany From 1992 to 2021) Tj T* (Claudia Winklmayr) Tj T* (Claudia Winklmayr, M.Sc.) Tj T* (1Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology, Robert Koch Institute \(RKI\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (Find articles by Claudia Winklmayr) Tj T* (1,*, Stefan Muthers) Tj T* (Stefan Muthers, Dr. phil.-nat.) Tj T* (2Research Centre Human Biometeorology, Deutscher Wetterdienst \(DWD\), Freiburg, Germany) Tj T* (Find articles by Stefan Muthers) Tj T* (2, Hildegard Niemann) Tj T* (Hildegard Niemann, Dr.-Ing.) Tj T* (3Department of Epidemiology and Health Monitoring, Robert Koch Institute \(RKI\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (Find articles by Hildegard Niemann) Tj T* (3, Hans-Guido Mücke) Tj T* (Hans-Guido Mücke, Dr. rer. nat.) Tj T* (4Department of Environmental Hygiene, German Environment Agency \(UBA\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (Find articles by Hans-Guido Mücke) Tj T* (4, Matthias an der Heiden) Tj T* (Matthias an der Heiden, Dr. rer. nat.) Tj T* (1Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology, Robert Koch Institute \(RKI\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (Find articles by Matthias an der Heiden) Tj T* (1) Tj T* (Author information) Tj T* (Article notes) Tj T* (Copyright and License information) Tj T* (1Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology, Robert Koch Institute \(RKI\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (2Research Centre Human Biometeorology, Deutscher Wetterdienst \(DWD\), Freiburg, Germany) Tj T* (3Department of Epidemiology and Health Monitoring, Robert Koch Institute \(RKI\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (4Department of Environmental Hygiene, German Environment Agency \(UBA\), Berlin, Germany) Tj T* (?*Abteilung für Infektionsepidemiologie Robert Koch-Institut Nordufer 20, 13353 Berlin, Germany winklmayrc@rki.de) Tj T* (Received 2021 Dec 21; Accepted 2022 Apr 13; Issue date 2022 Jul.) Tj T* (PMC Copyright notice) Tj T* (PMCID: PMC9639227  PMID: 35583101) Tj T* (See letter "Obesity is a Risk Factor" in volume 120 on page 188.) Tj T* (See letter "In Reply" in volume 120 on page 188.) Tj T* (See letter "Conceptual Misunderstanding" in volume 120 on page 188.) Tj T* (Abstract) Tj T* (Background) Tj T* (2018–2020 were unusually warm years in Germany, and the summer of 2018 was the second warmest summer since record-keeping began in 1881. Higher temper) Tj T* (  atures regularly lead to increased mortality, particularly among the elderly.) Tj T* (Methods) Tj T* (We used weekly data on all-cause mortality and mean temperature from the period 1992–2021 and estimated the number of heat-related deaths in all of Ge) Tj T* (  rmany, and in the northern, central, and southern regions of Germany, employing a generalized additive model \(GAM\). To characterize long-term trends) Tj T* (  , we compared the effect of heat on mortality over the decades.) Tj T* (Results) Tj T* (Our estimate reveals that the unusually high summer temperatures in Germany between 2018 and 2020 led to a statistically significant number of deaths ) Tj T* (  in all three years. There were approximately 8700 heat-related deaths in 2018, 6900 in 2019, and 3700 in 2020. There was no statistically significan) Tj T* (  t heat-related increase in deaths in 2021. A comparison of the past three decades reveals a slight overall decline in the effect of high temperature) Tj T* (  s on mortality.) Tj T* (Conclusion) Tj T* (Although evidence suggests that there has been some adaptation to heat over the years, the data from 2018–2020 in particular show that heat events rem) Tj T* (  ain a significant threat to human health in Germany.) Tj T* (Extreme heat and prolonged periods of heat are important risk factors for human health. Numerous studies have not only shown that high temperatures re) Tj T* (  sult in an increased burden on the health care system \(1, 2\), but also provided evidence of a systematic correlation between heat events and increas) Tj T* (  ed mortality \(3– 6\). For Germany, the effect of heat on mortality has been quantified both for individual federal states \(7– 12\) and nationwide \(3, ) Tj T* (  13– 16\).) Tj T* (High ambient temperatures have a variety of effects on the human body, for example reduced blood viscosity due to increased fluid loss, which exerts c) Tj T* (  onsiderable strain on the cardiovascular system, or the challenge to maintain a constant body temperature \(17, 18\). In particular, preexisting condi) Tj T* (  tions, such as diseases of the respiratory system, may be aggravated \(19, 20\). Since heat is rarely identified as a direct cause of death, statistic) Tj T* (  al methods have to be used to estimate the number of heat-related deaths. This article builds on the modeling approaches applied in studies on heat-) Tj T* (  related mortality between 1992 and 2017 as well as between 2001 and 2015 and estimates the number of heat-related deaths between 1992 and 2021, usin) Tj T* (  g a generalized additive model \(GAM\) \(21\).) Tj T* (Methods) Tj T* (Data) Tj T* (As in the 1992–2017 study \(3\), we used all-cause mortality data of the German Federal Statistical Office \(StBA, Statistisches Bundesamt\) per calendar ) Tj T* (  week in the period from 1992 to 2021 \(22\). These data are aggregated by four age groups \(<65, 65–74, 75–84, and 85+ years of age\) and by federal sta) Tj T* (  te. In addition, we made use of the StBA’s official population statistics as well as the results of the population projection for the year 2021 \(sce) Tj T* (  nario G2-L2-W2, assuming moderate developments in birth rate, life expectancy and net migration\) \(23\).) Tj T* (For the temperature data, we used hourly air temperature measurements of 52 stations of the surface observation network of the German Weather Service ) Tj T* (  \(Deutscher Wetterdienst, DWD\). These data were first averaged over the 24 hours of the day and then over calendar week and federal state. We also co) Tj T* (  nsidered the previously analyzed period 1992–2017, on the one hand, to ensure stable adaption of the seasonal pattern due to the longer observation ) Tj T* (  period, and, on the other hand, to allow for comparability with previous estimates. As before, we analyzed only the summer half-year \(calendar weeks) Tj T* (   15–40\) and distinguished the following three decades: 1992–2001, 2002–2011 and 2012–2021. Furthermore, we grouped the federal states into three maj) Tj T* (  or regions: “North” \(Bremen, Hamburg, Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania, Lower Saxony, Schleswig-Holstein\), “Central“ \(Berlin, Brandenburg, North Rhine-) Tj T* (  Westphalia, Rhineland-Palatinate, Saarland, Hesse, Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt, Thuringia\) and “South“ \(Baden-Wuerttemberg, Bavaria\). This approach allowe) Tj T* (  d us to take specific regional features of the effect of high temperatures on mortality into account.) Tj T* (Model) Tj T* (We used a generalized additive model \(GAM\) \(21\) and the R statistical software \(version 4.0.5, package “mgcv” [24]\) to model the curve of all-cause mo) Tj T* (  rtality observed during the study period. For each region and age group, the modelled all-cause mortality curve is composed of a long-term trend, a ) Tj T* (  seasonally recurring pattern and exposure-response curves which quantify the relative effect of mean temperature on mortality of the same week and t) Tj T* (  he following three weeks.) Tj T* (Based on the exposure-response curves, we identified temperature thresholds for each age group, region and decade above which temperature has a releva) Tj T* (  nt effect on mortality. We refer to a calendar week during which the mean temperature is above the threshold as a “heat week” and to contiguous peri) Tj T* (  ods of heat weeks as “heat periods”. Since the thresholds are close to 20 °C, we occasionally use this temperature value as an indicator of a heat w) Tj T* ET
endstream
