BT /F1 7 Tf 8.4 TL 28 812 Td (  eek.) Tj T* (We refer to the mortality to be expected if the weekly mean temperature always remained below the threshold as “background mortality”. The difference ) Tj T* (  between the modeled mortality curve and the background mortality yields the “heat-related mortality“. If the 95% confidence interval of the estimate) Tj T* (  d heat-related mortality is entirely above zero, we speak of a significant number of deaths. For a detailed description of the modelling used and th) Tj T* (  e sensitivity analyses conducted refer to the “Methods” eSupplement and \(3\).) Tj T* (Results) Tj T* (Heat-related deaths) Tj T* (Figure 1 shows the estimated number of heat-related deaths in Germany in the period 1992–2021. Between 2018 and 2020, heat-related deaths occurred in ) Tj T* (  significant numbers for three consecutive years for the first time during the study period. The year 2018, in particular, is with an estimated numbe) Tj T* (  r of about 8700 heat-related deaths similar in magnitude to the historical heat years 1994 and 2003 \(each about 10 000 deaths\). For the years 2019 a) Tj T* (  nd 2020, the model estimates approximately 6900 and 3700 deaths, respectively. The numbers of deaths are comparable to those in the years 2006, 2010) Tj T* (   and 2015. For 2021, no significant heat-related increase in \(all-cause\) mortality was found. The estimated numbers of deaths and confidence interva) Tj T* (  ls for the decade 2012–2021 are also summarized in the Table; the results for the entire period since 1992 are presented in the eTable.) Tj T* (Figure 1.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (Estimated number of heat-related deaths in Germany in the period 1992–2021. Years with significant numbers of heat-related deaths \(5% significance lev) Tj T* (  el\) are highlighted in red. Years with marginally significant numbers of heat-related deaths \(10% significance level\) are highlighted in beige. In a) Tj T* (  ddition, the estimated numbers of heat-related deaths with 95% confidence intervals are listed in the Table and eTable.) Tj T* (Table. The estimated numbers of heat-related deaths with 95% confidence intervals for the period 2012–2021**.) Tj T* (Year) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (Estimated number of heat-related deaths [95% confidence interval]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2012 ;) Tj T* (1000 [–1100; 3200] ;) Tj T* (2013) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (3000 [1100; 5100]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2014 ;) Tj T* (1000 [–1100; 2900] ;) Tj T* (2015) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (6000 [3900; 8200]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2016 ;) Tj T* (1800 [–600; 4 300] ;) Tj T* (2017 ;) Tj T* (1400 [–800; 3400] ;) Tj T* (2018) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (8700 [6700; 10 900]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2019) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (6900 [4600; 9300]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2020) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (3700 [1 400; 5600]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2021 ;) Tj T* (1700 [–700; 4300] ;) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (*Values in bold are statistically significant.) Tj T* (Figure 2 shows the development of mortality over time \(deaths per 100 000 population\) in the period 2018–2021. Between 2018 and 2020, in particular, b) Tj T* (  oth the modelled and the observed all-cause mortality was significantly higher than the modelled background mortality.) Tj T* (Figure 2.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (Mortality over time \(deaths per 100 000 population\) in the period 2018–2021. The gray line shows the reported all-cause mortality, the red line shows ) Tj T* (  the mortality estimated by the model \(only in the summer half-year\) and the blue line shows the estimated background mortality \(expected mortality w) Tj T* (  ithout heat\). Weeks with weekly mean temperatures \(averaged across all federal states\) above 20 °C are highlighted in yellow. The slightly increased) Tj T* (   all-cause mortality in spring 2020 and the significantly increased all-cause mortality in winter 2020/21 are explained by the first and second wave) Tj T* (   of the COVID-19 pandemic. A regional breakdown of the time series can be found in eFigure 1.) Tj T* (The eFigures 1 and 2 show a more detailed breakdown of mortality rates over time by the three regions \(North, Central, South\) and a comparison of heat) Tj T* (  -related mortality by region and age group. In line with previous findings \(3, 9, 15\), there is a clear indication that the group aged 85 years and ) Tj T* (  over is the most affected in all regions. The heat periods in the years 2018 and 2020 were shorter in the northern region, but still associated with) Tj T* (   a high number of heat-related deaths.) Tj T* (eFigure 1.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (The time series of mortality \(death per 100 000 population\) for the period 2018–2021, stratified by the northern, central and southern regions. The gr) Tj T* (  ay line shows the reported all-cause mortality, the red line shows the mortality estimated by the model \(only in the summer half-year\) and the blue ) Tj T* (  line shows the estimated background mortality. Weeks with weekly mean temperatures \(averaged across all federal states\) above 20°C are highlighted i) Tj T* (  n yellow. In the years 2018 to 2020, a shorter duration of the heat periods is noted in the northern region. The increased mortality in spring 2020 ) Tj T* (  in the southern region is explained by the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. The high mortality rates due to the second wave of the COVID-19 pand) Tj T* (  emic in the 2020/2021 winter were drawn beyond the limits of the y-axis to avoid distorting the depiction of the summer mortality.) Tj T* (eFigure 2.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (The heat-related mortality \(deaths per 100 000 population\) in the period 2018–2021, stratified by region and age group. Despite the shorter duration o) Tj T* (  f the heat periods \(efigure 1\) in the northern region, the heat-related mortality in the oldest age group in this region is comparable to the centra) Tj T* (  l and southern region mortality rates.) Tj T* (Figure 3 shows the shapes of the exposure-response curves of the current week and the previous week for the age group over 85 years in the three regio) Tj T* (  ns \(North, Central and South\) in the period 2012–2012. It can be seen here that the temperature threshold at which the effect of heat on mortality c) Tj T* (  an be considered relevant is somewhat lower in the northern region \(19.7 °C\) compared to the central region \(20.2 °C\) and southern region \(20.8 °C\).) Tj T* (   Moreover, in the northern region the exposure-response curves of the current week and the previous week show a considerably steeper rise for temper) Tj T* (  atures above the threshold. Thus, an effect of heat on mortality, which increases from the southern to the northern regions, is noted. Consequently,) Tj T* (   the expected number of heat-related deaths \(per 100 000 population of the same age group\) for a specific weekly mean temperature is somewhat higher) Tj T* (   in the northern region and somewhat lower in the southern region compared to the center of Germany.) Tj T* (Figure 3.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (Exposure-response curves of the current week \(dotted line\) and the previous week \(solid line\) for the 85+ age group in the period 2012–2012, stratifie) Tj T* (  d by the three regions “North“, “Central“ and “South“. In each case, the minimum of the exposure-response curve of the previous week is highlighted ) Tj T* (  with a black “x” and marks the temperature threshold above which both the temperature of the previous week and the temperature of the current week r) Tj T* (  esult in an increase in mortality. Temperature ranges above the threshold are highlighted in gray. The temperature threshold increases from north to) Tj T* ET
