BT /F1 7 Tf 8.4 TL 28 812 Td (   south.) Tj T* (Changes in the estimation of the exposure-response curves) Tj T* (Figure 4 shows the exposure-response curves for the age group 85 years by geographical region and decade. Overall, a significant increase in mortality) Tj T* (   is observed for weekly mean temperatures above 20 °C. Over the decades, a slight flattening of the curves can be observed, especially in the centra) Tj T* (  l region, i.e. in the period 2012–2021 the same weekly mean temperature resulted in a weaker increase in mortality compared to, for example, the per) Tj T* (  iod 1992–2001. A summary of the exposure-response curves for the various regions and age groups is provided in the “Analysis” eSupplement.) Tj T* (Figure 4.) Tj T* (Open in a new tab) Tj T* (The trend of the exposure-response curves for the three regions “North“, “Central“ and “South“ over the decades. The results for the 85+ age group are) Tj T* (   depicted because the strongest effects are observed in this age group. The three decades 1992 to 2001 \(red\), 2002 to 2011 \(blue\) and 2012 to 2021 \() Tj T* (  green\) show a slightly declining trend which is particularly evident in the central region. Since in the northern region weekly mean temperatures ab) Tj T* (  ove 25 °C occur significantly less frequently, the estimates in this area are associated with greater uncertainty, a fact that is reflected in the w) Tj T* (  ider confidence intervals.) Tj T* (Special features of the years 2018–2020 and the effect of heat period duration) Tj T* (Figure 2 and eFigure 1 show that the model can generally provide a good representation of mortality over time. However, mortality during the heat peri) Tj T* (  ods 2018 and 2020 is slightly underestimated, while it is slightly overestimated in 2019. This may be explained by differences in the character of t) Tj T* (  he heat waves in these three years.) Tj T* (In all three regions, the year 2018 was characterized by an unusually long heat period \(up to nine weeks in the southern region and up to five weeks i) Tj T* (  n the northern and central regions\). In addition, remarkably high weekly mean temperatures were measured \(up to 26.6 °C in the central and southern ) Tj T* (  regions; up to 25.1 °C in the northern region\). Although very high temperatures were also measured in 2019 \(maximum weekly mean temperatures of 25.8) Tj T* (   °C in the central region, 25.7 °C in the southern region and 25 °C in the northern region\), these heat periods were repeatedly interrupted by weeks) Tj T* (   with lower temperatures. Finally, in the year 2020 again a prolonged period of heat \(up to five weeks in the central and southern regions, up to th) Tj T* (  ree weeks in the northern region\) was observed; however, the maximum weekly mean temperature was significantly lower compared to the year 2018 \(maxi) Tj T* (  mum weekly mean temperature of 24.9 °C\).) Tj T* (The effect of explicitly including heat period duration in the model was assessed; however, taking this parameter into account did not result in a rel) Tj T* (  evant improvement of the description of the observed data. A detailed summary of key temperature figures during the study period is provided in the ) Tj T* (  “Data” eSupplement.) Tj T* (Discussion) Tj T* (In each of the years from 2018 to 2020, the number of heat weeks was higher compared to the numbers in the other years of the 2012–2021 decade. The ye) Tj T* (  ar 2018 particularly stands out, as not only did above-average periods of prolonged heat occur, but, in addition, exceptionally high temperatures we) Tj T* (  re measured. However, significant differences in the duration and number of heat periods are observed between the regions \(see also the “Data” eSupp) Tj T* (  lement\). In the period 2018–2020, heat-related mortality was also found to be considerably above the rates of the other years of the decade. In the ) Tj T* (  year 2018, the estimated number of heat-related deaths was comparable to those in the historical heat years 1994 and 2003. However, a direct compari) Tj T* (  son of mortality rates in the various weeks shows that despite comparable temperatures fewer deaths occurred in 2018 compared to 1994 and 2003 \(“Ana) Tj T* (  lysis” eSupplement\). In 2021, only sporadic heat weeks were observed which did not result in a significant increase in all-cause mortality.) Tj T* (Impact of the period 2018–2020 on the total decade) Tj T* (Since the exposure-response curves are estimated per decade, the inclusion of the comparably hot years 2018–2020 necessitated a re-estimation of the e) Tj T* (  xposure-response curves for the decade 2012–2021, which showed a steeper rise compared to the model based on the data up to 2017 \(3\). This implies t) Tj T* (  hat the estimated number of heat-related deaths for this decade also needs to be revised slightly upward. The updated estimates are within the 95% c) Tj T* (  onfidence intervals of the previous estimation \(see “Analysis” eSupplement\).) Tj T* (Duration of heat periods) Tj T* (By taking the duration of heat periods into account, only minor improvements in model fit were obtained and no significant changes in the estimates of) Tj T* (   heat-related deaths were noted. A possible explanation could be that calendar weeks as a unit are not fine enough to fully capture the time course ) Tj T* (  of a prolonged heat period. In particular, an alternation between hot and cooler days within a week and the magnitude of cooling at night cannot be ) Tj T* (  clearly differentiated based on weekly figures; for example, with the use of weekly data the duration of a heat period tends to be overestimated \(“D) Tj T* (  ata” eSupplement\). The analysis of daily mortality data could help to identify an optimum time unit.) Tj T* (Finally, phenomena other than temperature could also play a role, such as the occurrence and concentration of air pollutants, humidity and the positio) Tj T* (  n of a heat period in the calendar year \(25\). For example, in \(26\) it was shown that mortality during the first days of the heat waves in 2003 and 2) Tj T* (  015 differed significantly despite comparable temperature curves. These differences may be explained by the considerably higher humidity during the ) Tj T* (  2015 heat wave.) Tj T* (Comparison with other models) Tj T* (Some federal states, such as Hesse \(10\), Baden-Wuerttemberg \(11\), Berlin, and Brandenburg \(27\), publish estimates of the number of heat-related deaths) Tj T* (   on a regular basis. In these models, background mortality is calculated using the mean mortality rates of the previous years excluding periods with) Tj T* (   known heat events. The advantage of these models over the modelling approach used in our study is their ease of use, as they do not require any spe) Tj T* (  cialized statistical software. However, the challenge with these models is that, with extreme heat events occurring in increasing frequency, increas) Tj T* (  ingly longer periods of time must be excluded, thereby potentially confounding the estimated background mortality. Furthermore, the exposure-respons) Tj T* (  e relationship cannot be directly quantified, so temperature thresholds and adjustment processes must be described in some other way.) Tj T* (In addition, the topic of heat-related mortality is increasingly becoming the focus of international studies. In 2020, for example, the indicator “hea) Tj T* (  t-related mortality“ was included in the Lancet Countdown on Health and Climate Change. An estimated 296 000 heat deaths occurred worldwide in 2018,) Tj T* (   of which 20 000 were estimated to have occurred. in Germany alone \(6\). However, this estimate is based on the assumption of a globally uniform expo) Tj T* (  sure-response curve, and seasonal fluctuations in mortality were only broadly taken into account. While this simplified approach allows to estimate ) Tj T* (  heat-related mortality on a global scale, it may lead to considerable overestimation or underestimation in some countries \(with regard to the signif) Tj T* (  icance of regional differences, see also [28]\).) Tj T* (Adaption to heat periods in Germany) Tj T* (The development of the exposure-response curves over the decades, as shown in Figure 4, reveals that in general the effects of the same weekly mean te) Tj T* (  mperatures on mortality were weaker in the decade 2012–2021 compared to, for example, the decade 1992–2001. This may be interpreted as an indication) Tj T* (   that some adjustment to recurrent heat periods has taken place in the population.) Tj T* (The data analyzed by us do not allow to draw conclusions about what caused this limited adjustment. Possible explanations include individual behaviora) Tj T* (  l changes due to greater awareness, such as wearing light clothing, adequate hydration and moving to shaded or air-conditioned areas \(29\). For examp) Tj T* (  le, information about heat events is also made available by the Heat Health Warning System \(HHWS\) of the German Weather Service \(30\).) Tj T* (Since the elderly and those with preexisting conditions are affected most, the topic of heat prevention continues to be a focus in the health and care) Tj T* (   sectors, based on initial implementation experiences \(2\). When initiating adaptation strategies at the community level, coordination and interdisci) Tj T* (  plinary interaction is essential \(31, 32\). To this end, the 2020 Conference of Health Ministers emphasized the need for heat-health action plans \(“H) Tj T* (  itzeaktionsplan”\) in its relevant resolution, noting that these should be prepared by 2025 \(33\).) Tj T* (Outlook) Tj T* (Numerous studies suggest that the frequency of extreme heat events with, at times, dramatic effects on human health is likely to increase in Germany a) Tj T* (  s a result of climate change \(4, 20, 34– 38\). The study of heat-related mortality makes a significant contribution to the evaluation of health risks) Tj T* (  . Our updated analysis reveals for the first time significant numbers of heat-related death in three consecutive years, highlighting the fact that h) Tj T* (  eat events continue to be a serious threat to the health of the population in Germany. There is still an ongoing need and challenge to significantly) Tj T* (   improve the handling of heat periods in Germany and to adequately protect vulnerable groups of the population.) Tj T* (eTable. The estimated numbers of heat-related deaths with 95% confidence intervals for the period 1992–2021*.) Tj T* (Year) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (Estimated number of heat-related deaths [95% confidence interval]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (1992) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (2800 [600; 5000]) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (1993 ;) Tj T* (0 [–2600; 2 100] ;) Tj T* (1994) Tj T* (;) Tj T* (10 100 [8 100; 12 400]) Tj T* ET
